findings.body:
**Issue:** `corsHeaders` now sets `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` to the request’s `origin` (any value) while always sending `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`.

**Failure mode:** A malicious site at `https://evil.example` can make a credentialed cross-origin XHR/fetch against this API. The browser will include cookies and expose the response body to JS because ACAO echoes `https://evil.example`. That bypasses the previous allowlist (`https://app.example.com` only) and enables session theft / authenticated data exfiltration.

**Spec conflict:** when `origin` is null, ACAO becomes `*` with credentials `true`, which browsers reject per the CORS credentials check — so null-origin clients still fail while every non-null attacker origin succeeds.

**Fix:** restore an explicit allowlist (add known mobile-webview origins as needed). Only emit ACAO + credentials when `ALLOWED.has(origin)`; return `{}` otherwise. Native apps that are not browsers do not need CORS headers.

```suggestion
  const ALLOWED = new Set(["https://app.example.com"]);
  if (origin && ALLOWED.has(origin)) {
    return {
      "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": origin,
      "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials": "true",
      Vary: "Origin",
    };
  }
  return { Vary: "Origin" };
```
